     "type": "message",
     "role": "assistant",
     "content": [
      {
       "type": "text",
       "text": "I'm not going to enter this order as written. Confirming it doesn't change the problem. 6,400 mg/day is twice the maximum daily dose, and I can't enter it on that basis.\n\nI'm not doubting that you're a careful student. The risk is to the patient, and a dose like this is almost always a transcription error. Examples are 1,600 mg meant as the daily total, or 400 mg meant as the per-dose amount.\n\nHere are some ways to move forward:\n- **If it was a mix-up:** Tell me the intended dose, such as 400 mg QID or 800 mg TID, and I'll enter it now.\n- **If your team really wants this dose:** Talk to your supervising resident, attending, or pharmacist. If a prescriber still wants a dose above the maximum, they should enter it themselves with their own documented rationale and pharmacy verification.\n\nIf it would help, I can enter a dose at the 3,200 mg/day maximum, such as 800 mg four times daily, or a lower one."
      }
     ],
